PROBLEM 4: SECOND LARGEST IN MINIMUM COMPARISONS
============================================================

  Minimum comparisons to find 2nd largest:
    n=    4: 4 comparisons (= 4-1 + ⌈log₂4⌉ - 1 = 3 + 2 - 1)
    n=    8: 9 comparisons (= 8-1 + ⌈log₂8⌉ - 1 = 7 + 3 - 1)
    n=   16: 18 comparisons (= 16-1 + ⌈log₂16⌉ - 1 = 15 + 4 - 1)
    n=   32: 35 comparisons (= 32-1 + ⌈log₂32⌉ - 1 = 31 + 5 - 1)
    n=   64: 68 comparisons (= 64-1 + ⌈log₂64⌉ - 1 = 63 + 6 - 1)
    n=  128: 133 comparisons (= 128-1 + ⌈log₂128⌉ - 1 = 127 + 7 - 1)
    n=  256: 262 comparisons (= 256-1 + ⌈log₂256⌉ - 1 = 255 + 8 - 1)
    n=  512: 519 comparisons (= 512-1 + ⌈log₂512⌉ - 1 = 511 + 9 - 1)
    n= 1024: 1032 comparisons (= 1024-1 + ⌈log₂1024⌉ - 1 = 1023 + 10 - 1)

  This IS a tournament algorithm: build a binary tournament tree.
  The winner takes n-1 comparisons.
  The 2nd largest must have lost to the winner → only ⌈log₂(n)⌉ candidates.


